- UG | ask the eldest mage for the oath's oldest wording, and swear that instead | door | binds | v: tradition, universalism | chance: 55
- WR | swear, and tell the hall plainly which clause you will never turn on the people | grants: a name for straight talk | binds | v: benevolence, universalism | chance: 85
- WU | petition the Order through the proper channel for a gentler wording | grants: drafting policy | v: conformity, universalism | chance: 35
works: The oath settles, and {N} takes up the office with a clear sense of what it holds.
works: Months later {N} feels the oath only as a quiet weight, the way an old ring feels on a finger.
fails: The hall murmurs, and the Order's mages exchange a long look that {N} does not like.
fails: The words sit badly from the first day, and {N} wakes more than once with them burning in {Ns} mouth.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 71. a rival's curse (pairs D1b)

# Round 5, 2026-10-07 (P14): value tags read from the act on 2 options (check.py section 4).
== a rival's curse
tier: life event
only: magic
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-95
holds: member of parliament | minister | mayor | party leader
per_year: 0 0 .01 .01 .01 .01
drivers: unrest+.3 trouble+.15
times: per office-holder in the magic world a year: about 1 in 100 (estimate)
likelier: a bitter rivalry, a close vote coming, hedge-witches for hire in the city, wards on the house that have lapsed
rarer: the Order's wards on the house, an office-holder with few enemies
tone: trouble
life: work, health
horizon: months
roles: rival, partner, colleague, friend
magic: your voice fails in the chamber three days running, and a hex-mark is found under your door
scene magic: For three days running {Ns} voice dies the moment {N} rises to speak in the chamber. On the fourth morning a servant finds a hex-mark scratched under the door of the house, and everyone knows who has wanted {Ns} seat.
scene magic W: There is a court for exactly this. Laying a curse on an office-holder of the realm is a crime, and crimes have names and punishments.
scene magic U: {N} copies the hex-mark carefully onto paper. Every hand that draws such marks has its habits, and habits can be traced.
scene magic B: {N} knows what the curse is for: to make {N} look weak before the vote. The question is how to make it cost {rival} more than it costs {N}.
scene magic R: {N} is furious, and hoarse, and more determined than ever to stand up in that chamber every single day.
scene magic G: {N} thinks of the old healer beyond the walls, who knows the herbs for fevers and the salts for hexes, and of home, where nobody has to speak at all.
- W | take the hex-mark to the Order's court, and name the rival though you lack proof | mark: made an enemy | closed: approval: an accusation without proof; backfire: the court clears the rival, and the accuser pays the costs | v: conformity, security | chance: 50
- U | trace the hex-mark to whoever drew it, and take the proof to the Order | v: self-direction, security | chance: 65
- B | pay a hedge-witch to turn the curse back on the rival | mark: hid a wrong | closed: law: buying a curse against an office-holder; backfire: the Order's court, and the office in danger | v: power | chance: 65
- R | rise in the chamber every day anyway, cracked voice and all | habit | v: self-direction, stimulation | self_control: + | chance: 70
- G | go to the old healer beyond the walls for a counter-charm of herbs and salt | v: tradition | chance: 65
- RG | go home to your district, and tell your own people the story yourself | grants: good name in town | v: benevolence, tradition | chance: 70
- UB | let it be known the hex has failed, and watch who in the faction flinches | v: power, security | chance: 70
- UR | have a friend read out your speech mocking the hex, and dare the rival again | grants: rousing a crowd | v: stimulation, self-direction | chance: 65
- WB | ask the faction to ward your house and lend you a stand-in for the votes | v: power | chance: 80
- WG | give up your offices with dignity, and wait out the hex at home | drops: member of parliament; mayor | mark: gave in to pressure | v: security, tradition | self_control: - | chance: 90
works: Within a month {Ns} voice is back, and the matter is settled the way {N} chose.
works: The story of the hex goes round the city, and most of the sympathy goes to {N}.
fails: The hex holds through the vote, and {N} sits silent while the bill passes.
fails: The matter drags on for months, and {rival} smiles every time {N} rises to speak.
